1283 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 95 0 R/T(stack from overflowing. Memoisation removes the repeated work but not the depth: ways_memo\(1000\) calls ways_memo\(999\), which calls ways_memo\(998\), and so on, so the stack is about 1,000 deep before the first value is returned. In my run n = 500 still worked and n = 1000 failed. )/E(stack from overflowing. Memoisation removes the repeated work but not the depth: ways_memo\(1000\) calls ways_memo\(999\), which calls ways_memo\(998\), and so on, so the stack is about 1,000 deep before the first value is returned. In my run n = 500 still worked and n = 1000 failed. )/Pg 30 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1284 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 95 0 R/T(Options tested: \(A\) raising the limit to 5000 works \(209-digit answer\) but only moves the ceiling and risks a real stack overflow for very large n; \(B\) rewriting with a loop that keeps two values works for any n with O\(1\) space and is fastest \(0.000029 s against 0.000214 s for memo at n = 1000\); \(C\) filling the cache bottom-up \(calling ways_memo\(100\), \(200\)...\) keeps recursion shallow. Decision: use the loop \(B\) for deployment, up to 1,000 steps and beyond, because it has no depth limit, no cache memory and the simplest failure behaviour. I would not raise the limit as the only fix. I would keep the memoised recursive version only as the readable, tested reference implementation to verify the loop against \(they agree: True\), and raise the limit only if recursion must be kept, together with the memo. )/E(Options tested: \(A\) raising the limit to 5000 works \(209-digit answer\) but only moves the ceiling and risks a real stack overflow for very large n; \(B\) rewriting with a loop that keeps two values works for any n with O\(1\) space and is fastest \(0.000029 s against 0.000214 s for memo at n = 1000\); \(C\) filling the cache bottom-up \(calling ways_memo\(100\), \(200\)...\) keeps recursion shallow. Decision: use the loop \(B\) for deployment, up to 1,000 steps and beyond, because it has no depth limit, no cache memory and the simplest failure behaviour. I would not raise the limit as the only fix. I would keep the memoised recursive version only as the readable, tested reference implementation to verify the loop against \(they agree: True\), and raise the limit only if recursion must be kept, together with the memo. )/Pg 30 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

