endobj

1344 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 96 0 R/T(What changed: the recursion is replaced by a bottom-up loop. Property exploited: ways\(n\) depends only on ways\(n-1\) and ways\(n-2\), so only the last two values are ever needed \(overlapping sub-problems with optimal substructure\). Extra memory: none beyond two variables \(a memoised version would need an n-entry cache and n stack frames\). Trade-offs: \(1\) the code is less obviously a copy of the mathematical definition, so readability and traceability fall; \(2\) it computes every smaller value even when only one n is needed, which is the same work as memoisation; \(3\) intermediate results are not kept, so repeated queries for different n recompute from the beginning \(a cache or a list would fix this at O\(n\) space\); \(4\) the big integers grow \(about n x 0.69 bits\), so for very large n the additions are no longer constant-time. )/E(What changed: the recursion is replaced by a bottom-up loop. Property exploited: ways\(n\) depends only on ways\(n-1\) and ways\(n-2\), so only the last two values are ever needed \(overlapping sub-problems with optimal substructure\). Extra memory: none beyond two variables \(a memoised version would need an n-entry cache and n stack frames\). Trade-offs: \(1\) the code is less obviously a copy of the mathematical definition, so readability and traceability fall; \(2\) it computes every smaller value even when only one n is needed, which is the same work as memoisation; \(3\) intermediate results are not kept, so repeated queries for different n recompute from the beginning \(a cache or a list would fix this at O\(n\) space\); \(4\) the big integers grow \(about n x 0.69 bits\), so for very large n the additions are no longer constant-time. )/Pg 31 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

