1971 0 obj
<</Title(Same inputs, same results, countsand timesforboth versions. The hand-written dictionary memo \(alsovalidated, True\) needs 2n-1 calls \(59 for n=30\). The redesign alone: n = 1,000 in 0.00068 s with 476 bytes of peakextra memory \(209 digits\); n = 10,000in0.00842 s;n=100,000 in 0.14344 s \(20,898 digits\) - the time is slightlyworse than linear at the largest n becauseof big-integeraddition, as noted in the trade-offs.)/Dest[33 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1674 0 R/Prev 2053 0 R/Next 1789 0 R>>
endobj

1972 0 obj
<</Title(Objective achieved? Yes. At n = 30 the work fell from 2,692,537 calls to 29 additions \(the predicted 93,000x\), the time ratio was about 17,000x \(below the operation ratio because Python's call overhead is only part of the original's time, but far above my 100x minimum\); n = 1000 took 0.00068 s \(< 0.01 s\) and 476 bytes; no recursion limit applies; all results are identical to the original. Size of improvement: exponential to linear. New costs: the loop is less self-explanatory than the definition, it stores no history \(repeated queries recompute\), and for n above about 105 big-integer additions make it slower than linear. Where the original may still be preferable: for n up to about 20 \(0.001 s\) the plain recursion is instantly fast, matches the mathematical definition line for line and is the clearest teaching and verification reference \(I use it to check the redesign\); and when a table of all ways\(k\) values for k up to n is wanted, memoised recursion yields them as a by-product. Conclusion: deploy the loop for the trust's 1,000-step staircases, and keep the recursive version as the readable specification and as a test oracle for small n.)/Dest[33 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1789 0 R>>
endobj

