2259 0 obj
<</Title(D. When is a while loop more natural, and what bug appears if the counter is updated in the wrong place?)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2256 0 R/First 2261 0 R/Last 2261 0 R/Count -1/Next 2262 0 R>>
endobj

2260 0 obj
<</Title(def redesign\(n\):)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2263 0 R/Next 2257 0 R>>
endobj

2261 0 obj
<</Title(Awhile loop isnatural when the number of validattempts is not fixed in advance: blank entries must be ignored, and \(see the changed condition\) the limit itself can change during the loop. A for loop with range\(3\) fixes the count at the start. If the counter is updated before the blank check, a blank entry consumes an attempt: the demo ['', '', '', '482913'] gave VERIFIED with the correct design but LOCKED with the misplaced counter, so a customer who pressed Enter three times was locked out before typing the OTP. Updating the counter after a successful break check \(or both before the loop test\) has the opposite risk of allowing extra attempts.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2259 0 R>>
endobj

2262 0 obj
<</Title(E. Changed condition: two extra attempts after a correct security answer)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2259 0 R/First 2264 0 R/Last 2265 0 R/Count -2/Next 2266 0 R>>
endobj

2263 0 obj
<</Title(if n<= 1: return 1)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2267 0 R/Next 2260 0 R>>
endobj

2264 0 obj
<</Title(Afor loop over range\(3\)cannot grow, so a whileloopwithavariablelimit isthe rightdesign: limit = 3; when attempts reaches the limit for the first time and the security question is answered correctly, limit += 2 \(granted)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2262 0 R/Next 2265 0 R>>
endobj

2265 0 obj
<</Title(once only, so the customer cannot gain endless attempts\). Tested: 5 wrong answers with a correct security answer ends LOCKED after 5 attempts; a wrong security answer locks after 3; a correct OTP on the 5th attempt is VERIFIED.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2262 0 R/Prev 2264 0 R>>
endobj

2266 0 obj
<</Title(F. Alternative concept: recursion)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2262 0 R/First 2268 0 R/Last 2269 0 R/Count -4/Next 2270 0 R>>
endobj

2267 0 obj
<</Title(#unchanged Q6)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2271 0 R/Next 2263 0 R>>
endobj

2268 0 obj
<</Title(Version)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2266 0 R/First 2272 0 R/Last 2273 0 R/Count -2/Next 2274 0 R>>
endobj

2269 0 obj
<</Title(Both are linear in time; recursion is about 8-9x slower here because of function-call overhead and uses stack depth equal to the number of attempts \(maximum depth 4 for 3 wrong attempts\). With a = 3000 and the default limit of 1000, the recursive version raised RecursionError, while the loop has no such limit. For three attempts the difference is irrelevant, so the loop is preferred.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2266 0 R/Prev 2275 0 R>>
endobj

