<</Title(Stringsareimmutable: once created, the charactersof a str object cannot be changed \(t[0]='T' raised TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment\). This lets Python share strings safely, cache hashes \(so strings work as dictionary keys\) and intern them. text.upper\(\) therefore cannot modify text; it builds a new string and returns it. The output shows the original is still 'tn09ab1234' and the result is a different object, so the result must be assigned: text = text.upper\(\).)/Dest[20 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2317 0 R/Next 2329 0 R>>
endobj

2329 0 obj
<</Title(s[-4:] is the last four characters for any length, so it gives '1234' for both the 9-character 'TN09A1234' and the 10- character 'TN09AB1234'. s[4:-4] starts after the first four characters and stops before the last four, leaving the series: 'A' \(1 character\) for the 9-character number and 'AB' \(2 characters\) for the 10-character one. This is why the same slices work for both lengths.)/Dest[20 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2317 0 R/Prev 2328 0 R>>
endobj

