2368 0 obj
<</Title(Average of three runs: += 0.0127 s, join 0.0059 s \(join about 2.2x faster at 200,000 lines; best-of-3 timing inside one run gives 5.6x\).)/Dest[22 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2356 0 R/Prev 2379 0 R>>
endobj

2369 0 obj
<</Title(The results at 200,000 lines \(bestof3\): += 0.01034 s, f-string += 0.01103s, StringIO 0.00865 s, join 0.00186 s: join is about 5.6x faster. My prediction of the order was right, but I predicted a quadratic blow-up for += and did not see one: going from 10,000 to 200,000 lines \(20x\) cost about 22x more time, which is linear. The reason is that CPython sometimes optimises +=: when the string has only one reference, the interpreter can resize the string in place \(realloc\) instead of copying, so each += is amortised cheap. Immutability is still the reason join is the safer practice: I defeated the optimisation by keeping a second reference to the old string, and the time exploded: 100,000 lines took 6.0625 s against 0.0069 s for plain += \(about 880x slower\), and doubling from 50,000 to 100,000 lines multiplied the time by about 11x \(more than 4x per doubling\), the O\(n2\) signature of copying. The optimisation is an implementation detail of CPython \(absent in PyPy, Jython, and not applicable when the string is an attribute, a list element, or shared\), so ''.join\(\) remains the recommended practice: it is O\(n\) in every implementation and in every situation.)/Dest[22 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2357 0 R>>
endobj

